/*
    GrayScottResonator
    ==================

    A morphing resonator built on a 1D Gray-Scott reaction-diffusion ring
    lattice that is integrated at audio rate.

        dU/dt = Du ∇²U - U V² + F (1 - U)
        dV/dt = Dv ∇²V + U V² - (F + K) V

    Signal flow (per sample, per channel):

        in ──tanh──×drive──► B-spline splat into V at the injector
                                   │
                [ 128-node ring, explicit Euler, 1-8 sub-steps, soft-clamped,
                  Resonance as anti-damping around the homogeneous state ]
                                   │
        pickup (cubic B-spline) ◄──┘
          └─► DC block ─► 2-pole LP ─► fixed gain ─► level match ─► tanh ─► dry/wet ─► gain ─► out

    How it resonates: between the Hopf and saddle-node curves of the F×K
    plane the homogeneous state (U*, V*) is a stable focus, so the lattice
    rings around it like a tuned resonator (Q → ∞ at the Hopf curve; past it
    the ring self-oscillates). The lattice sits exactly on that state when it
    exists, and on the trivial state (U=1, V=0) when it doesn't, so with no
    input there is nothing to hear: every sound starts from the input. Left of
    the Hopf curve the state is unstable and the ring oscillates by itself
    once disturbed; Resonance ≥ ~1 does the same anywhere near the focus.

    Level: how loudly the lattice answers varies by ~40 dB across the settings,
    so the output is level-matched to the input, outside the feedback loop. A
    linear model of the ring predicts the gain for each setting as soon as it
    moves (see "The level model" below), and a trim learned from the input over
    about half a second makes up the model's error (the input's spectrum, and
    how far loud input pushes the ring out of its linear range). The trim
    freezes while there is no input, so it can't lift noise, stretch tails, or
    pump. Before the model, the match had only the learned part: after a change
    the level took a second or more to come back, and where the ring is quiet
    it ran into its +30 dB ceiling and stayed low. (An AGC inside the loop did
    pump: it raised the loop gain whenever things went quiet, turning the ring
    into a relaxation oscillator that clicked on its own clock.)

    Resonance is anti-damping inside the chemistry, not an audio feedback loop:
    each step adds g·A·d/(A + |d|) to V, with d = V − V*. That raises the
    Jacobian's trace by g, so the focus rings longer at nearly the same pitch;
    the saturation at A turns what would be Gray-Scott's subcritical Hopf
    (a growing ring swings out of the focus's basin and collapses) into a small,
    stable limit cycle. g is normalised so Resonance means the same thing
    everywhere on the band: 0 is the natural damping, 1 is exactly the edge of
    oscillation for the integrator as it runs, and above 1 the ring sustains
    itself (see updateAntiDamping). The GUI's pitch and ring-time readouts do the
    same arithmetic (gui/src/Macro.res). Audio feedback from the pickups, which
    this replaced, couldn't keep the pitch: the lattice has a lot of gain near
    DC, so the loop found a lower mode of its own (106 Hz where the focus was at
    345 Hz).

    Time is normalised to a 48 kHz reference, so a given Speed gives the same
    pitch at any host sample rate.

    Stability (see STABILITY.md for the derivation):
      * The discrete Laplacian is L = ½(x[i-1] + x[i+1]) - x[i], whose eigenvalues
        lie in [-2, 0]. With Du ≤ 0.5 and dt ≤ 1 we have Du·dt·2 ≤ 1, so the
        diffusion part of explicit Euler is monotone (no sign-flipping
        checkerboard modes) — not just stable.
      * F ∈ [0.010, 0.090], K ∈ [0.045, 0.070] keep every reaction-Jacobian
        eigenvalue at the trivial state well inside the Euler stability disc.
      * Each node is soft-clamped into [0, 1.25] after every step; NaN and -Inf
        map to 0 and +Inf to 1.25, so the lattice can never hold a non-finite
        value. An output watchdog re-seeds the lattice as a last resort.
*/

namespace GrayScott
{
    let numNodes = 128;
    let maxSubSteps = 8;          // Speed 4 at 24 kHz still gets dt ≤ 1
    let maxStep = 1.25f;          // longest Euler step, in lattice time
    let referenceRate = 48000.0f; // the lattice's time base

    /// Peak V added per sample at an injector for a full-scale input at Drive
    /// 0 dB. Small enough that the ring stays a linear resonator around its
    /// focus, so it rings at the pitch the GUI shows; Drive (up to ×16)
    /// pushes it out of that into nonlinear, pattern-breaking territory.
    let injectionGain = 0.003f;

    /// Amplitude (in V) at which Resonance's anti-damping saturates. Small
    /// enough that a self-oscillating ring stays well inside the focus's basin.
    let resonanceLimit = 0.004f;

    /// Furthest a pickup sits from its injector, in nodes. At audio rates a
    /// diffusive lattice only carries a signal ~√(2D/ω) ≈ 3-4 nodes before it is
    /// attenuated by e, so the useful range is short; beyond it you mostly hear
    /// the lattice's own slow dynamics.
    let maxPickupDistance = 12.0f;

    /// Fixed gain from V fluctuation to the feedback path and the level match.
    /// At the default settings the lattice already answers at about the input's
    /// level, so this is close to unity.
    let wetGain = 1.5f;



    /// Most the level match will boost the wet signal (+50 dB), and cut it (−12 dB).
    /// At the default settings the ring answers ~35 dB below the input, and up to
    /// ~45 dB with the pickups far out or a high pitch.
    let maxLevelMatch = 316.0f;
    let minLevelMatch = 0.25f;

    /// How far the learned trim may move the predicted level (±18 dB).
    let maxTrim = 8.0f;

    /// The level model (see the processor's matchLevel): the even spatial modes
    /// 0, 2, … 64, which are all a mono input excites (the injectors are half the
    /// ring apart), and the frequencies it averages over, 20 Hz to 20 kHz.
    let modelModes = 33;
    let modelPoints = 96;

    /// The model takes Resonance as no more than this. Real input saturates the
    /// anti-damping (it's soft-limited at resonanceLimit), so the closer to the
    /// edge, the more the linear gain overstates what you hear; 0.7 fits renders
    /// of music-level pink noise best, and the trim makes up the rest.
    let modelResonanceCap = 0.7f;

    /// While the input is quieter than this (≈ −60 dBFS) the lattice is never
    /// re-seeded, so nothing happens without input.
    let inputPresentThreshold = 0.001f;

    /// Snapshot of the lattice sent to the GUI, ~30 times a second.
    struct LatticeFrame
    {
        float[numNodes] v;
        float injectL, injectR;   // node positions (fractional)
        float tapL, tapR;
        float level;              // smoothed |wet| level, 0..1
    }

    //==============================================================================
    processor Resonator  [[ main ]]
    {
        input  stream float<2> in;
        output stream float<2> out;

        input event
        {
            float feed            [[ name: "Feed (F)",          min: 0.010, max: 0.090, init: 0.04462 ]];
            float kill            [[ name: "Kill (K)",          min: 0.045, max: 0.070, init: 0.0585 ]];
            float diffusionU      [[ name: "Diffusion U",       min: 0.05,  max: 0.50,  init: 0.40 ]];
            float diffusionRatio  [[ name: "Diffusion Ratio",   min: 0.10,  max: 1.00,  init: 1.00 ]];
            float speed           [[ name: "Speed",             min: 0.05,  max: 4.00,  init: 1.00 ]];
            float drive           [[ name: "Drive",             min: 0.0,   max: 24.0,  init: 0.0,  unit: "dB" ]];
            float tapDistance     [[ name: "Pickup Distance",   min: 0.0,   max: 1.0,   init: 0.3 ]];
            float feedback        [[ name: "Resonance",         min: 0.0,   max: 1.5,   init: 0.93 ]];
            float mix             [[ name: "Mix",               min: 0.0,   max: 1.0,   init: 1.0 ]];
            float outputGain      [[ name: "Output",            min: -36.0, max: 12.0,  init: -6.0, unit: "dB" ]];
        }

        /// Re-seeds the lattice for the current F/K (not a host parameter).
        input event void reseed;

        /// Lattice snapshots for the visualiser.
        output event LatticeFrame lattice;

        //==============================================================================
        event feed           (float x)  { feedT     = clamp (x, 0.010f, 0.090f); }
        event kill           (float x)  { killT     = clamp (x, 0.045f, 0.070f); }
        event diffusionU     (float x)  { duT       = clamp (x, 0.05f, 0.50f); }
        event diffusionRatio (float x)  { ratioT    = clamp (x, 0.10f, 1.00f); }
        event speed          (float x)  { speedT    = clamp (x, 0.05f, 4.00f); }
        event drive          (float x)  { driveT    = dbToGain (clamp (x, 0.0f, 24.0f)); }
        event tapDistance    (float x)  { distT     = clamp (x, 0.0f, 1.0f); }
        event feedback       (float x)  { fbT       = clamp (x, 0.0f, 1.5f); }
        event mix            (float x)  { mixT      = clamp (x, 0.0f, 1.0f); }
        event outputGain     (float x)  { gainT     = dbToGain (clamp (x, -36.0f, 12.0f)); }
        event reseed()                  { seedLattice(); }

        //==============================================================================
        // Lattice state: each node's U and V as offsets from a reference state
        // (uRef, vRef), the steady state the ring rings around. Near rest the
        // offsets are tiny, and so is their rounding: kept as concentrations (~0.3)
        // every step rounded to ~3e-8, which a high-Q focus kept ringing as a steady
        // tone after the sound had died away. Offsets decay to true silence.
        float[numNodes] uOff, vOff;
        float uRef = 1.0f, vRef;
        float[numNodes] lapU, lapV;  // scratch for integrate

        // Parameter targets (set by events) and their smoothed per-sample values
        float feedT = 0.04462f, killT = 0.0585f, duT = 0.40f, ratioT = 1.0f, speedT = 1.0f,
              driveT = 1.0f, distT = 0.3f, fbT = 0.93f, mixT = 1.0f, gainT = 0.5f;

        float feedS = 0.04462f, killS = 0.0585f, duS = 0.40f, ratioS = 1.0f, speedS = 1.0f,
              driveS = 1.0f, distS = 0.3f, fbS = 0.93f, mixS = 1.0f, gainS = 0.5f;

        float smoothCoeff;
        float rateScale;             // referenceRate / sample rate

        // Sub-steps per sample and their length, for the Speed and Diffusion they were worked out at
        int latticeSteps;
        float latticeDt, stepsSpeed = -1.0f, stepsDu = -1.0f;

        // Resonance: anti-damping g·(V − V*), updated every 64 samples (V* is vRef)
        float antiDamping;
        int framesUntilFocusUpdate;

        // Output conditioning
        float<2> dcX1, dcY1, lpZ1, lpZ2;
        float dcCoeff, lpB0, lpB1, lpB2, lpA1, lpA2;
        float levelFollower;

        // Level match (outside the feedback loop): the level the model predicts,
        // times a trim learned from the input
        float inputPower, wetPower, trim = 1.0f;
        float powerCoeff, matchCoeff;
        float predicted = 1.0f, predictedS = 1.0f;

        // The level model, worked through a mode or a frequency per sample
        float[modelModes] modeWeight, modeP11, modeTrace, modeDet;
        float[modelPoints] pointCos, pointSin, pointChain;
        float[numNodes] ringCos, ringSin;
        int modelStage;              // < modelModes: a mode; then a frequency; then idle
        float modelPower;
        float modelF, modelK, modelDu, modelRatio, modelSpeed, modelFb, modelDist, modelDrive;
        float modelU, modelV, modelG, modelDt;
        int modelSteps;

        // Revival: when the field has collapsed to the trivial (silent) state in
        // a region where a homogeneous resonant state exists, and there is
        // input to resonate, the ring is re-seeded and the wet path fades back in.
        float latticeMass;           // Σ V over the ring, every 64 samples
        float inputEnvelope, inputRelease;
        int deadSamples;
        int reviveAfterSamples;
        float wetFade = 1.0f;
        float wetFadeStep;

        int framesUntilSnapshot;
        int snapshotInterval;

        //==============================================================================
        void init()
        {
            let fs = float (processor.frequency);
            rateScale = referenceRate / fs;

            // ~25 ms one-pole parameter glide: kills zipper noise on F/K/tap moves
            smoothCoeff = 1.0f - exp (-1.0f / (0.025f * fs));

            // 8 Hz DC blocker — the V field sits on a large, slowly moving mean
            dcCoeff = 1.0f - (2.0f * float (pi) * 8.0f / fs);

            // 2nd-order Butterworth low-pass at min(16 kHz, 0.42·fs).
            // The lattice's Nyquist-rate (checkerboard) content and the
            // harmonics of the U·V² nonlinearity are removed before output.
            let fc = min (16000.0f, 0.42f * fs);
            let k = tan (float (pi) * fc / fs);
            let q = 0.70710678f;
            let norm = 1.0f / (1.0f + k / q + k * k);
            lpB0 = k * k * norm;
            lpB1 = 2.0f * lpB0;
            lpB2 = lpB0;
            lpA1 = 2.0f * (k * k - 1.0f) * norm;
            lpA2 = (1.0f - k / q + k * k) * norm;

            reviveAfterSamples = int (0.3f * fs);
            inputRelease = 1.0f - exp (-1.0f / (0.2f * fs));
            powerCoeff = 1.0f - exp (-1.0f / (0.5f * fs));
            matchCoeff = 1.0f - exp (-1.0f / (0.25f * fs));
            wetFadeStep = 1.0f / (0.06f * fs);

            snapshotInterval = max (1, int (fs / 30.0f));
            framesUntilSnapshot = snapshotInterval;

            initLevelModel (fs);
            seedLattice();
        }

        //==============================================================================
        void main()
        {
            loop
            {
                smoothParameters();

                // Lattice time per sample, in Euler sub-steps (see subSteps), worked out
                // again only while Speed or Diffusion moves
                if (speedS != stepsSpeed || duS != stepsDu)
                {
                    stepsSpeed = speedS;
                    stepsDu = duS;
                    let latticeTime = speedS * rateScale;
                    latticeSteps = subSteps (latticeTime, duS);
                    latticeDt = stepLength (latticeTime, latticeSteps, duS);
                }

                let du = duS;
                let dv = duS * ratioS;

                // Ring geometry: inputs enter at ¼ and ¾ of the ring, pickups are
                // mirrored away from them (L clockwise, R anticlockwise).
                let quarter = float (numNodes) * 0.25f;
                let injL = quarter;
                let injR = 3.0f * quarter;
                let dist = distS * maxPickupDistance;
                let tapL = injL + dist;
                let tapR = injR - dist;

                if (--framesUntilFocusUpdate <= 0)
                {
                    framesUntilFocusUpdate = 64;
                    updateAntiDamping (latticeDt);
                    latticeMass = sum (vOff) + float (numNodes) * vRef;
                    flushTinyOffsets();
                }

                // Excitation: the soft-saturated input, scaled by Drive
                let x = in;
                let excL = driveS * tanh (x[0]);
                let excR = driveS * tanh (x[1]);

                // The excitation is added straight into V, once per sample.
                splat (injL, injectionGain * excL);
                splat (injR, injectionGain * excR);

                for (int s = 0; s < latticeSteps; ++s)
                    integrate (latticeDt, feedS, killS, du, dv);

                trackInput (x);
                checkForCollapse();

                // Spatial read-out through a cubic B-spline (low-pass) kernel
                var wet = float<2> (readTap (tapL), readTap (tapR));

                let raw = lowPass (dcBlock (wet)) * wetGain;

                if (! isFiniteVector (raw))
                {
                    seedLattice();
                    wet = float<2>();
                }
                else
                {
                    wet = tanh (raw * matchLevel (x, raw)) * wetFade;
                }

                stepLevelModel();

                wetFade = min (1.0f, wetFade + wetFadeStep);

                let level = 0.5f * (abs (wet[0]) + abs (wet[1]));
                levelFollower += (level - levelFollower) * (level > levelFollower ? 0.01f : 0.0005f);

                out <- (x * (1.0f - mixS) + wet * mixS) * gainS;

                if (--framesUntilSnapshot <= 0)
                {
                    framesUntilSnapshot = snapshotInterval;
                    sendSnapshot (injL, injR, tapL, tapR);
                }

                advance();
            }
        }

        //==============================================================================
        /// One explicit-Euler step of the reaction-diffusion system, in two passes
        /// over the ring: the Laplacians from the values before the step, then each
        /// node's update on its own. Both are plain loops the C++ compiler keeps in
        /// registers, with no branch but the clamp at 0; the knee at the top of
        /// softClamp only runs when something has reached it (strong Drive), so the
        /// usual step skips 256 calls that would change nothing.
        ///
        /// With u = U* + a and v = V* + b, and U* V*² = F (1 − U*) = (F + K) V* at the
        /// reference, the reaction in offsets is
        ///     da/dt = −F a − s,   db/dt = s − (F + K) b,   s = U v² − U* V*²
        ///                                                    = U* (2V* + b) b + a v²
        /// which is the same Euler step, just without the reference's rounding.
        void integrate (float dt, float F, float K, float du, float dv)
        {
            lapU[0] = 0.5f * (uOff[numNodes - 1] + uOff[1]) - uOff[0];
            lapV[0] = 0.5f * (vOff[numNodes - 1] + vOff[1]) - vOff[0];

            for (int i = 1; i < numNodes - 1; ++i)
            {
                lapU.at (i) = 0.5f * (uOff.at (i - 1) + uOff.at (i + 1)) - uOff.at (i);
                lapV.at (i) = 0.5f * (vOff.at (i - 1) + vOff.at (i + 1)) - vOff.at (i);
            }

            lapU[numNodes - 1] = 0.5f * (uOff[numNodes - 2] + uOff[0]) - uOff[numNodes - 1];
            lapV[numNodes - 1] = 0.5f * (vOff[numNodes - 2] + vOff[0]) - vOff[numNodes - 1];

            // Resonance's anti-damping, g·A·d/(A + |d|) with d = V − V* (= b): linear
            // for small deviations, so it sharpens the focus's Q, but bounded for
            // large ones, so a self-oscillating ring settles into a limit cycle of
            // about ±A instead of swinging out of the focus's basin (Gray-Scott's Hopf
            // bifurcation is subcritical) and collapsing to the trivial state. The
            // gradual roll-off matters: a hard clamp keeps pushing at full strength
            // past ±A, and the ring collapses anyway.
            let gA = dt * antiDamping * resonanceLimit;
            let limit = resonanceLimit;
            let U0 = uRef;
            let V0 = vRef;
            let dtU0 = dt * U0;
            let twoV0 = 2.0f * V0;
            let decayU = 1.0f - dt * F;
            let decayV = 1.0f - dt * (F + K);
            let lowestU = 1.0e-12f - U0;   // an offset that takes a concentration below 0
            let lowestV = 1.0e-12f - V0;
            let dtDu = dt * du;
            let dtDv = dt * dv;
            var highestU = 0.0f;
            var highestV = 0.0f;

            for (wrap<numNodes> i)
            {
                let a = uOff[i];
                let b = vOff[i];
                let v = V0 + b;
                let reaction = dtU0 * (twoV0 + b) * b + dt * a * v * v;
                let an = decayU * a + dtDu * lapU[i] - reaction;
                let bn = decayV * b + dtDv * lapV[i] + reaction + gA * b / (limit + abs (b));
                // the bottom of softClamp: a negative concentration (or NaN) becomes 0
                let ak = an >= lowestU ? an : -U0;
                let bk = bn >= lowestV ? bn : -V0;
                uOff[i] = ak;
                vOff[i] = bk;
                highestU = max (highestU, ak);
                highestV = max (highestV, bk);
            }

            if (U0 + highestU > 0.9f || V0 + highestV > 0.9f)
            {
                for (wrap<numNodes> i)
                {
                    uOff[i] = clampOffset (uOff[i], U0);
                    vOff[i] = clampOffset (vOff[i], V0);
                }
            }
        }

        /// A decaying ring's offsets would end in denormals, which are slow on x86
        /// (and x − c·x rounds back to x down there, so they'd stay). Every 64 samples
        /// is often enough: an offset takes far longer than that to fall from 1e-20
        /// to the denormal range.
        void flushTinyOffsets()
        {
            for (wrap<numNodes> i)
            {
                uOff[i] = abs (uOff[i]) < 1.0e-20f ? 0.0f : uOff[i];
                vOff[i] = abs (vOff[i]) < 1.0e-20f ? 0.0f : vOff[i];
            }
        }

        /// softClamp for an offset from a reference, which leaves an offset alone
        /// (no rounding through the reference) wherever softClamp would.
        float clampOffset (float offset, float reference)
        {
            let x = reference + offset;

            if (x > 0.9f || ! (x >= 1.0e-12f))
                return softClamp (x) - reference;

            return offset;
        }

        /// Keeps a concentration physical. Identity on [1e-12, 0.9], then a C¹
        /// tanh knee asymptotic to 1.25. Everything else — negatives
        /// (concentrations can't be negative), denormal-range values and NaN,
        /// which fails both comparisons — becomes 0.
        float softClamp (float x)
        {
            let knee = 0.9f;
            let headroom = 0.35f;

            if (x > knee)
                return knee + headroom * tanh ((x - knee) / headroom);

            if (x >= 1.0e-12f)
                return x;

            return 0.0f;
        }

        //==============================================================================
        /// Uniform cubic B-spline basis. Sums to 1, C² continuous and strictly
        /// positive, so moving a pickup/injector is click-free and the kernel
        /// acts as a spatial anti-aliasing low-pass (it attenuates the
        /// lattice's Nyquist mode by a factor of 3).
        float<4> bsplineWeights (float t)
        {
            let t2 = t * t;
            let t3 = t2 * t;
            let it = 1.0f - t;

            return float<4> ((it * it * it) / 6.0f,
                             (3.0f * t3 - 6.0f * t2 + 4.0f) / 6.0f,
                             (-3.0f * t3 + 3.0f * t2 + 3.0f * t + 1.0f) / 6.0f,
                             t3 / 6.0f);
        }

        float readTap (float position)
        {
            let p = wrapPosition (position);
            let base = int (floor (p));
            let w = bsplineWeights (p - float (base));

            return w[0] * vOff.at (base - 1)
                 + w[1] * vOff.at (base)
                 + w[2] * vOff.at (base + 1)
                 + w[3] * vOff.at (base + 2);
        }

        /// Adds a bipolar amount of V around a position. The clamp keeps the
        /// concentration physical when the input pulls it below zero.
        void splat (float position, float amount)
        {
            let p = wrapPosition (position);
            let base = int (floor (p));
            let w = bsplineWeights (p - float (base));

            vOff.at (base - 1) = clampOffset (vOff.at (base - 1) + w[0] * amount, vRef);
            vOff.at (base)     = clampOffset (vOff.at (base)     + w[1] * amount, vRef);
            vOff.at (base + 1) = clampOffset (vOff.at (base + 1) + w[2] * amount, vRef);
            vOff.at (base + 2) = clampOffset (vOff.at (base + 2) + w[3] * amount, vRef);
        }

        float wrapPosition (float p)
        {
            let n = float (numNodes);
            return p - n * floor (p / n);
        }

        //==============================================================================
        void smoothParameters()
        {
            feedS  = glide (feedS,  feedT);
            killS  = glide (killS,  killT);
            duS    = glide (duS,    duT);
            ratioS = glide (ratioS, ratioT);
            speedS = glide (speedS, speedT);
            driveS = glide (driveS, driveT);
            distS  = glide (distS,  distT);
            fbS    = glide (fbS,    fbT);
            mixS   = glide (mixS,   mixT);
            gainS  = glide (gainS,  gainT);
        }

        /// One sample of a parameter's glide, landing exactly on the target once
        /// it's within 1e-6 of it. A glide towards 0 would otherwise end in
        /// denormals and stay there (x − c·x rounds back to x), and on x86 every
        /// operation on a denormal is slow: with Resonance at 0, the smoothed value
        /// is multiplied in at every node of every step, which doubled the cost.
        float glide (float smoothed, float target)
        {
            let d = target - smoothed;
            return abs (d) < 1.0e-6f ? target : smoothed + d * smoothCoeff;
        }

        float<2> dcBlock (float<2> x)
        {
            let y = x - dcX1 + dcCoeff * dcY1;
            dcX1 = x;
            dcY1 = y;
            return y;
        }

        float<2> lowPass (float<2> x)
        {
            // Transposed direct form II
            let y = lpB0 * x + lpZ1;
            lpZ1 = lpB1 * x - lpA1 * y + lpZ2;
            lpZ2 = lpB2 * x - lpA2 * y;
            return y;
        }

        /// The gain that brings the wet signal to the input's loudness: what the
        /// level model predicts for these settings, glided over ~25 ms, times a trim
        /// learned while there is input (for its spectrum, and for how far it pushes
        /// the ring out of its linear range). The prediction moves as soon as a
        /// setting does, so changing one doesn't leave you waiting for the level;
        /// the trim carries over from one setting to the next and only has the
        /// model's error to learn. It freezes without input, so it can't lift noise,
        /// stretch tails or pump.
        float matchLevel (float<2> x, float<2> raw)
        {
            predictedS = glide (predictedS, predicted);

            if (inputEnvelope >= inputPresentThreshold)
            {
                let heard = raw * predictedS;
                inputPower += (0.5f * (x[0] * x[0] + x[1] * x[1]) - inputPower) * powerCoeff;
                wetPower   += (0.5f * (heard[0] * heard[0] + heard[1] * heard[1]) - wetPower) * powerCoeff;
                let target = clamp (sqrt (inputPower / max (wetPower, 1.0e-12f)), 1.0f / maxTrim, maxTrim);
                trim += (target - trim) * matchCoeff;
            }

            return clamp (predictedS * trim, minLevelMatch, maxLevelMatch);
        }

        //==============================================================================
        // The level model
        //
        // How loud the lattice answers varies by ~40 dB across the settings. This
        // predicts it: the ring linearised around its steady state is 64 pairs of
        // spatial modes, each a 2×2 linear system, so the wet path's response to a
        // mono input is a sum of second-order sections,
        //     H(z) = Σ_q c_q · (P11 − det P · z⁻¹) / (1 − tr P · z⁻¹ + det P · z⁻²)
        // where P is one sample of the mode's dynamics (its Euler step to the power
        // of the sub-step count) and c_q how strongly the injectors and the left
        // pickup couple to it. Its RMS gain for pink noise (equal power per octave,
        // 20 Hz to 20 kHz), with the DC blocker and the low-pass, is what the wet
        // path is divided by. On the Play page's resonant band it lands within ~2 dB
        // of renders; elsewhere, and with heavy Drive, the trim does more of the work.
        //
        // It's recomputed only when a setting has moved, a mode or a frequency per
        // sample: about 130 samples of work, and nothing while settings stay put.

        void initLevelModel (float fs)
        {
            for (wrap<numNodes> k)
            {
                let angle = 2.0f * float (pi) * float (k) / float (numNodes);
                ringCos[k] = cos (angle);
                ringSin[k] = sin (angle);
            }

            let lo = 20.0f;
            let hi = min (20000.0f, 0.45f * fs);

            for (wrap<modelPoints> j)
            {
                let hz = lo * pow (hi / lo, (float (j) + 0.5f) / float (modelPoints));
                let w = 2.0f * float (pi) * hz / fs;
                pointCos[j] = cos (w);
                pointSin[j] = -sin (w);
                // |DC blocker · low-pass|² here, both fixed
                let zi = complex32 (pointCos[j], pointSin[j]);
                let one = complex32 (1.0f, 0.0f);
                let dc = (one - zi) / (one - zi * dcCoeff);
                let lp = (lpB0 + zi * lpB1 + zi * zi * lpB2) / (one + zi * lpA1 + zi * zi * lpA2);
                let chain = dc * lp;
                pointChain[j] = chain.real * chain.real + chain.imag * chain.imag;
            }

            startLevelModel();

            while (modelStage <= modelModes + modelPoints)
                stepLevelModel();

            predictedS = predicted;
        }

        /// Takes the settings the model works on: the targets, so it runs once for a
        /// change rather than all through the glide (predictedS does the gliding).
        /// Resonance is taken capped, so a move above the cap doesn't rerun it.
        void startLevelModel()
        {
            modelF = feedT;
            modelK = killT;
            modelDu = duT;
            modelRatio = ratioT;
            modelSpeed = speedT;
            modelFb = min (fbT, modelResonanceCap);
            modelDist = distT;
            modelDrive = driveT;

            let latticeTime = modelSpeed * rateScale;
            modelSteps = subSteps (latticeTime, modelDu);
            modelDt = stepLength (latticeTime, modelSteps, modelDu);

            let state = homogeneousState (modelF, modelK);
            modelU = state[0];
            modelV = state[1];
            modelG = modelFb * edgeGain (modelF, modelK, modelV, modelDt);

            modelStage = 0;
            modelPower = 0.0f;
        }

        bool near (float a, float b)    { return abs (a - b) <= 1.0e-4f * abs (b); }

        bool settingsMoved()
        {
            return ! (near (modelF, feedT) && near (modelK, killT) && near (modelDu, duT)
                       && near (modelRatio, ratioT) && near (modelSpeed, speedT)
                       && abs (modelFb - min (fbT, modelResonanceCap)) <= 1.0e-3f
                       && abs (modelDist - distT) <= 1.0e-3f);
        }

        void stepLevelModel()
        {
            if (modelStage < modelModes)
                setUpMode (modelStage);
            else if (modelStage < modelModes + modelPoints)
                addFrequency (modelStage - modelModes);
            else if (modelStage == modelModes + modelPoints)
                finishLevelModel();
            else
            {
                // Drive only scales the result, so it needn't run the model again
                if (settingsMoved())
                    startLevelModel();
                else if (modelDrive != driveT)
                {
                    modelDrive = driveT;
                    finishLevelModel();
                }

                return;
            }

            ++modelStage;
        }

        /// Mode q = 2m: one sample of its dynamics, and its coupling to the ring's
        /// injectors and left pickup.
        void setUpMode (int m)
        {
            let q = 2 * m;
            let lap = ringCos.at (q) - 1.0f;
            let dt = modelDt;
            let F = modelF;
            let K = modelK;
            let U = modelU;
            let V = modelV;

            // one Euler step of the linearised reaction-diffusion, then the sub-steps
            let a00 = 1.0f + dt * (-V * V - F + modelDu * lap);
            let a01 = dt * (-2.0f * U * V);
            let a10 = dt * (V * V);
            let a11 = 1.0f + dt * (2.0f * U * V - (F + K) + modelG + modelDu * modelRatio * lap);
            var p00 = a00;
            var p01 = a01;
            var p10 = a10;
            var p11 = a11;

            for (int s = 1; s < modelSteps; ++s)
            {
                let n00 = p00 * a00 + p01 * a10;
                let n01 = p00 * a01 + p01 * a11;
                let n10 = p10 * a00 + p11 * a10;
                let n11 = p10 * a01 + p11 * a11;
                p00 = n00;
                p01 = n01;
                p10 = n10;
                p11 = n11;
            }

            var trace = p00 + p11;
            var det = p00 * p11 - p01 * p10;

            // A mode that grows (past the edge, or a node near the saddle-node curve)
            // is taken as just stable: what you hear from it is set by the
            // saturation the model leaves out.
            let disc = trace * trace * 0.25f - det;

            if (disc < 0.0f)
            {
                if (det > 0.998f)
                {
                    trace *= sqrt (0.998f / det);
                    det = 0.998f;
                }
            }
            else
            {
                let r1 = clamp (trace * 0.5f + sqrt (disc), -0.999f, 0.999f);
                let r2 = clamp (trace * 0.5f - sqrt (disc), -0.999f, 0.999f);
                trace = r1 + r2;
                det = r1 * r2;
            }

            modeP11.at (m) = p11;
            modeTrace.at (m) = trace;
            modeDet.at (m) = det;

            // the injectors at ¼ and ¾ of the ring put in the same (each splatted
            // on its node); the left pickup reads at ¼ + distance
            let injector = numNodes / 4;
            let injection = bsplineWeights (0.0f);
            let tap = float (injector) + modelDist * maxPickupDistance;
            let base = int (floor (tap));
            let w = bsplineWeights (tap - float (base));
            var tapRe = 0.0f;
            var tapIm = 0.0f;
            var injRe = 0.0f;
            var injIm = 0.0f;

            for (int j = 0; j < 4; ++j)
            {
                let t = (q * (base - 1 + j)) & (numNodes - 1);
                tapRe += w.at (j) * ringCos.at (t);
                tapIm += w.at (j) * ringSin.at (t);
                let i = (q * (injector - 1 + j)) & (numNodes - 1);
                injRe += 2.0f * injection.at (j) * ringCos.at (i);
                injIm -= 2.0f * injection.at (j) * ringSin.at (i);
            }

            // mode q and its mirror −q together, as a real weight
            let pair = (q == 0 || q == numNodes / 2) ? 1.0f : 2.0f;
            modeWeight.at (m) = pair * (tapRe * injRe - tapIm * injIm) / float (numNodes);
        }

        void addFrequency (int j)
        {
            let zi = complex32 (pointCos.at (j), pointSin.at (j));
            let zi2 = zi * zi;
            let one = complex32 (1.0f, 0.0f);
            var y = complex32 (0.0f, 0.0f);

            for (wrap<modelModes> m)
                y += modeWeight[m] * (modeP11[m] - zi * modeDet[m]) / (one - zi * modeTrace[m] + zi2 * modeDet[m]);

            modelPower += (y.real * y.real + y.imag * y.imag) * pointChain.at (j);
        }

        void finishLevelModel()
        {
            let gain = injectionGain * modelDrive * wetGain * sqrt (modelPower / float (modelPoints));
            predicted = clamp (1.0f / max (gain, 1.0e-9f), minLevelMatch, 1000.0f);
        }

        /// Sub-steps per sample: each as long as diffusion allows, Du·dt ≤ ½ (which
        /// keeps it monotone, STABILITY.md), and no more than 1.25, which keeps the
        /// reaction's Euler error small. At the default Diffusion that's one step up
        /// to Speed 1.25, so a 44.1 kHz host doesn't need two steps where 48 kHz
        /// needs one. If even maxSubSteps can't keep up (sample rates below 24 kHz),
        /// the lattice runs slow rather than breaking the bound.
        int subSteps (float latticeTime, float du)
        {
            return clamp (int (ceil (latticeTime / longestStep (du))), 1, maxSubSteps);
        }

        float stepLength (float latticeTime, int steps, float du)
        {
            return min (longestStep (du), latticeTime / float (steps));
        }

        float longestStep (float du)    { return min (maxStep, 0.5f / du); }

        /// Resonance as anti-damping, normalised so Resonance 1 is exactly the
        /// edge of oscillation for the integrator as it runs (dt per sub-step).
        /// Explicit Euler multiplies a mode by z = 1 + λ·dt, which grows by about
        /// ω²·dt/2 per step on its own, so the edge is where |z| = 1, not where
        /// Re λ = 0. Adding g to the Jacobian's V–V entry makes the trace
        /// trace + g and the determinant det − g·(V*² + F), and |z|² = 1 solves to
        ///     g_edge = −(trace + det·dt) / (1 − (V*² + F)·dt).
        /// None where there's no stable focus to sharpen.
        void updateAntiDamping (float dt)
        {
            let state = homogeneousState (feedS, killS);
            moveReference (state[0], state[1]);
            antiDamping = fbS * edgeGain (feedS, killS, vRef, dt);
        }

        /// g_edge for the focus at F, K (V is its V*), or 0 where there's none.
        float edgeGain (float F, float K, float V, float dt)
        {
            if (V <= 0.0f)
                return 0.0f;

            let v2 = V * V;
            let trace = K - v2;
            let det = (F + K) * (v2 - F);
            return max (0.0f, -(trace + det * dt) / (1.0f - (v2 + F) * dt));
        }

        /// Takes a new reference state (F or K has moved), keeping the concentrations
        /// where they are. The pickups read offsets, so the DC blocker's memory moves
        /// with them and doesn't hear the change of reference as a step.
        void moveReference (float u, float v)
        {
            if (u != uRef || v != vRef)
            {
                let du = uRef - u;
                let dv = vRef - v;

                for (wrap<numNodes> i)
                {
                    uOff[i] += du;
                    vOff[i] += dv;
                }

                dcX1 += dv;
                uRef = u;
                vRef = v;
            }
        }

        bool isFiniteVector (float<2> x)
        {
            // NaN fails every comparison, ±Inf fails the magnitude test
            return abs (x[0]) < 1.0e6f && abs (x[1]) < 1.0e6f;
        }

        //==============================================================================
        /// The upper-branch homogeneous steady state (U*, V*) of the reaction, or
        /// (1, 0) if it doesn't exist. From U V² = F(1-U) and U V = F + K:
        ///     (F+K) V² - F V + F(F+K) = 0
        /// which has real roots only below the saddle-node curve K = √F/2 - F.
        /// Between the Hopf curve (V*² = K) and the saddle-node curve this state
        /// is a stable focus: the lattice rings like a tuned resonator around it.
        float<2> homogeneousState (float F, float K)
        {
            let FK = F + K;
            let disc = F * F - 4.0f * F * FK * FK;

            if (disc <= 0.0f)
                return float<2> (1.0f, 0.0f);

            let vStar = (F + sqrt (disc)) / (2.0f * FK);
            return float<2> (FK / vStar, vStar);
        }

        /// Puts the ring exactly on the steady state for the current F/K: the
        /// homogeneous resonant state where it exists, otherwise the trivial
        /// state. Both are equilibria, so the ring stays silent until the input
        /// disturbs it. The output filters start from rest.
        void seedLattice()
        {
            let state = homogeneousState (feedT, killT);
            uRef = state[0];
            vRef = state[1];

            for (wrap<numNodes> i)
            {
                uOff[i] = 0.0f;
                vOff[i] = 0.0f;
            }

            dcX1 = float<2>();
            dcY1 = float<2>();
            lpZ1 = float<2>();
            lpZ2 = float<2>();
            deadSamples = 0;
            wetFade = 0.0f;
        }

        void trackInput (float<2> x)
        {
            let peak = max (abs (x[0]), abs (x[1]));
            inputEnvelope = peak > inputEnvelope ? peak : inputEnvelope + (peak - inputEnvelope) * inputRelease;
        }

        /// Re-seeds a ring that has collapsed to the silent trivial state, but only
        /// while there is input to resonate and only where a resonant state exists.
        void checkForCollapse()
        {
            if (latticeMass > 1.0e-3f || inputEnvelope < inputPresentThreshold)
            {
                deadSamples = 0;
                return;
            }

            if (++deadSamples > reviveAfterSamples)
            {
                if (homogeneousState (feedS, killS)[1] > 0.0f)
                    seedLattice();
                else
                    deadSamples = 0;
            }
        }

        void sendSnapshot (float injL, float injR, float tapL, float tapR)
        {
            LatticeFrame frame;
            for (wrap<numNodes> i)
                frame.v[i] = vRef + vOff[i];

            frame.injectL = injL;
            frame.injectR = injR;
            frame.tapL = wrapPosition (tapL);
            frame.tapR = wrapPosition (tapR);
            frame.level = levelFollower;
            lattice <- frame;
        }

        float dbToGain (float db)   { return pow (10.0f, db * 0.05f); }
    }
}
